                    briefing_text_r1:       `Three well-funded programmes in eight years delivered technology and almost no value — only <strong>22% of the last one is actually used</strong>, and it over-ran €9M. Peppard's central argument: realising value from technology is a <strong>leadership and organizational challenge, not a technical one</strong>. Resist the reflex to blame the vendors, the budget, or the users. Separate "technology delivered" from "value realized", name the organizational root causes — no business ownership of outcomes, no adoption or change effort, an IT function built to run projects not realize benefits — and define what <em>value</em> means in measurable terms the board can see.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Prioritize & sequence the initiative portfolio`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `The €30M cannot fund everything. Build a sequenced two-year roadmap that balances a <strong>visible Year-1 win</strong> — to keep the board funding the programme — against <strong>foundational capability</strong> that pays off later but unblocks everything. Respect the dependencies: you <em>cannot</em> run mobile self-service on a frozen 34-year-old mainframe, and the renewals portal already corrupted 14,000 records on that foundation. Choose what comes first, and pick an early win that proves value without waiting two years for a re-platform.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Design the IT function & split the €30M`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Two coupled decisions. First, redesign the IT <strong>operating model</strong>: shift from a project-delivery, order-taking shop toward a product-and-value organization — and decide <em>who is accountable for the benefit</em> of each initiative, IT or the business. Second, split the €30M across <strong>paying down technical debt</strong> (cutting the 18 incidents a quarter and freeing the 60% of engineering time lost to legacy), <strong>building new capability</strong> (the initiatives that win business), and <strong>change &amp; adoption</strong> (the missing third that the last programmes never funded). Too much debt and the board sees no value; too little and new capability is built on sand.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Commit & show how value is realized`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present the full plan to the board chair and the Central Bank reviewer. Show not just <em>what</em> you will build but <strong>how value will actually be realized</strong> — the named business owners, the adoption plan, and the benefit-tracking metrics the last three programmes lacked. Answer the regulator: a credible 90-day remediation that makes the renewals failure <strong>structurally impossible</strong> is your transformation case, not a bolt-on memo. The board will press: is this real value, or another technology spend dressed up? Defend that value is a leadership challenge, not a purchase.`,

