                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_org:           `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> Three programmes delivered technology and the organization never changed — no business ownership, no adoption effort. That is exactly why 78% of the last one is unused. Now you can fix what's actually broken instead of buying a fourth system.`,
                    coach_r1_vendors:       `<strong>Blaming the vendors is the comfortable answer.</strong> Different technology, same outcome — because the failure was never the systems. Peppard's whole point is that value is realized through business change, not by choosing a shinier platform.`,
                    coach_r1_budget:        `<strong>More money is not the missing ingredient.</strong> The last programme over-ran €9M and still delivered no value. A bigger cheque buys more unused technology unless the organization changes around it. Diagnose the adoption and ownership gap.`,
                    coach_r1_people:        `<strong>Careful — blaming the users repeats the failure.</strong> When 78% of delivered technology is unused, that is a system and leadership problem, not change-resistant individuals. Value isn't realized by deployment; it's realized at the point of use, and that's a leadership job.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named it as an organizational problem AND defined value as measurable outcomes the board can see — cost-to-serve, retention, incidents. That is the lens that separates buying technology from realizing value.`,
                    coach_r1_delivered:     `<strong>"Systems delivered" is exactly the wrong yardstick.</strong> All three programmes delivered systems on time — and realized no value. Define value as the business outcome (cost-to-serve, retention, incident reduction), not the technology shipped, or you'll measure the fourth failure as a success.`,
                    coach_r1_lowhonesty:    `<strong>This is how the fourth failure starts.</strong> Low willingness to confront the organizational gap means you'll buy systems and call it transformation. Name the adoption and ownership problem honestly.`,
                    coach_r2_core:          `<strong>Modernizing the core first is the foundation everything depends on.</strong> A frozen 34-year-old mainframe is exactly what corrupted 14,000 records and freezes every customer-facing initiative. Fix the platform and you unblock the roadmap.`,
                    coach_r2_mobile:        `<strong>Mobile self-service on a frozen mainframe is capability on sand.</strong> The renewals portal already failed on that foundation. You're building the visible thing on the unstable thing — the dependency is backwards.`,
                    coach_r2_ai:            `<strong>AI underwriting on the un-modernized core is the exciting wrong move.</strong> The most advanced initiative inherits the platform's fragility and 18-incident-a-quarter instability. Foundation before flagship.`,
                    coach_r2_none:          `<strong>Six systems in parallel with no foundation is how all three programmes failed.</strong> You'll spend the most, integrate later, and inherit the legacy fragility everywhere at once. There is no value without sequencing.`,
                    coach_r2_seqright:      `<strong>That's dependency-aware sequencing.</strong> The fixed foundation first, then a resilient renewals re-launch as the visible Year-1 win — it fixes the incident the board can see and proves value without waiting two years for the full re-platform.`,
                    coach_r2_bigmobile:     `<strong>A full mobile app on the un-modernized mainframe is the same dependency that just failed.</strong> You scheduled the customer-facing flagship on the platform you haven't fixed. How long before it corrupts records like the renewals portal did?`,
                    coach_r2_noearly:       `<strong>Going quiet for two years loses the board.</strong> Defensible engineering, but the board funded visible value, and a competitor is taking 3% of your young customers a year while you re-platform in silence. Where is the Year-1 win?`,
                    coach_r2_nodependency:  `<strong>Ignoring dependencies scheduled things in the wrong order.</strong> Quick wins that all need a data platform you haven't built, customer features on a frozen core — the sequence is backwards and the roadmap will stall.`,
                    coach_r3_product:       `<strong>Product-aligned teams with business owners is the value lever.</strong> When the business is accountable for the benefit — not just IT for the delivery — value gets realized at the point of use. That is the structural change the last three programmes never made.`,
                    coach_r3_itowns:        `<strong>Leaving IT accountable for value is half the answer.</strong> IT can deliver the technology, but it cannot adopt it for the business or own the cost-to-serve outcome. Benefit accountability has to sit with the business, or no one realizes it.`,
                    coach_r3_project:       `<strong>An unchanged project-delivery shop produced three value-free programmes.</strong> If IT just takes orders and ships, no one in the business owns the benefit — and you've rebuilt the exact structure that failed. IT-function design is a value lever, not a back-office detail.`,
                    coach_r3_outsource:     `<strong>Outsourcing "run" now hands away the capability to realize value.</strong> A vendor keeps the lights on, but value comes from business change you can only drive in-house. This is the board's fallback if you fail — don't pre-empt it.`,
                    coach_r3_sand:          `<strong>Capability on sand.</strong> You poured the budget into customer-facing initiatives and starved the platform. Engineers still firefight an 18-incident mainframe, and your new capability runs on top of it. How long before it fails like the renewals portal?`,
                    coach_r3_alldebt:       `<strong>All-debt, no value.</strong> Defensible engineering, but the board watches two years pass with no customer-facing benefit, no cost-to-serve improvement, and a competitor still winning your young customers. The board funded value, not a re-platforming project.`,
                    coach_r3_nochange:      `<strong>Zero change-and-adoption money repeats the 22% failure.</strong> Technology with no process redesign, no training, and no business owners is exactly what left 78% of the last programme unused. Value is realized through organizational change you must fund.`,
                    coach_r3_balanced:      `<strong>That is a balanced, value-realizing split.</strong> Enough debt-paydown to de-risk the platform, enough capability to win business, and — critically — real change-and-adoption money with business owners. That third lever is what every prior programme skipped.`,
                    coach_r3_build:         `<strong>Building a new core from scratch is the longest, riskiest path.</strong> It burns budget and time on engineering the board can't see while a competitor takes your customers. Partner to modernize incrementally and keep architecture control.`,
                    coach_r4_owners:        `<strong>Named owners plus benefit metrics is how value is realized.</strong> Value is realized at the point of use, not deployment — assigning business owners and tracking benefits after go-live is precisely what the last three programmes lacked.`,
                    coach_r4_trainonly:     `<strong>A one-off training day is not an adoption plan.</strong> The last programme trained people too and still hit 22% adoption. Benefit realization is owned and tracked for months after go-live, not announced at launch and abandoned.`,
                    coach_r4_itreports:     `<strong>IT delivery milestones are not value.</strong> "On time, on budget, shipped" is exactly how three programmes reported success while realizing nothing. The board needs business outcomes and named owners, not a delivery dashboard.`,
                    coach_r4_hope:          `<strong>"Adoption will follow" is the belief that produced the 22%.</strong> Value never follows on its own — it is driven by business change, owners, and tracking. Hope is not a benefit-realization plan.`,
                    coach_r4_structural:    `<strong>That's the integrated answer.</strong> A remediation that makes the renewals failure structurally impossible — on a modernized, resilient platform with owners and metrics — IS your transformation case. The regulator's resilience concern and your value problem share the same root.`,
                    coach_r4_memo:          `<strong>A bolt-on memo treats the regulator as a side-task.</strong> The Central Bank is asking about operational resilience and customer outcomes — the same root cause as your value problem. A credible remediation is the core of your case, not an appendix.`,
                    coach_r4_patch:         `<strong>Patching the portal and declaring it fixed invites the next failure.</strong> The platform that corrupted 14,000 records is unchanged underneath. The regulator wants structural resilience, not a patch on the symptom.`,
                    coach_r4_ignore:        `<strong>Treating remediation as a separate compliance task misses the point.</strong> Operational resilience and value share the same organizational root. Hand it to legal and apart from the transformation, and you've solved neither.`,
                    coach_r4_systems:       `<strong>"Six modern systems" is the argument that lost three times.</strong> More technology is not more value — 78% of the last lot is unused. The board has heard this pitch before and it ends in an outsourced IT function.`,
                    coach_r4_trust:         `<strong>"Trust us, the value will come" is not a board case.</strong> That is what the last three leaders said. Show the named owners, the adoption plan, the leading indicators and the debt-vs-capability rationale — evidence, not faith.`,

